%N A230543 Numbers k that form a Pythagorean quadruple with k', k'' and sqrt(k^2 + k'^2 + k''^2), where k' and k'' are the first and the second arithmetic derivative of k.
%C A230543 130000000000 < a(41) <= 244140625000. - _Carlo Corti_, Oct 12 2026
%H A230543 Carlo Corti, <a href="/A230543/b230543.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..40</a>
%H A230543 Carlo Corti, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23311392">An Exact Local Extension and Homothetic Classes for OEIS A230543</a>, 2026.
%H A230543 Carlo Corti, <a href="/A230543/a230543.txt">Table of n, a(n), a(n)', a(n)'', and the diagonal for n = 1..40</a>
%H A230543 Jurij Kovič, <a href="https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL15/Kovic/kovic4.html">The Arithmetic Derivative and Antiderivative</a>, J. Integer Seq. 15 (2012), Article 12.3.8.
%H A230543 Victor Ufnarovski and Bo Åhlander, <a href="https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL6/Ufnarovski/ufnarovski.html">How to Differentiate a Number</a>, J. Integer Seq. 6 (2003), Article 03.3.4.
%H A230543 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/PythagoreanQuadruple.html">Pythagorean Quadruple</a>
%F A230543 Writing h(i) = A051674(i), the numbers 2*h(i)*h(j)*h(k)*h(l), 99*h(i)*h(j)*h(k), 25557426*h(i)*h(j), and 4802517313*h(i) are terms for i,j,k,l >= 1; indices may repeat. - _Carlo Corti_, Oct 12 2026
%e A230543 If k = 6336 then k' = 23808, k'' = 103936 and sqrt(k^2 + k'^2 + k''^2) = 106816.
%p A230543 with(numtheory): P:= proc(q) local a1, a2, n, p;
%p A230543 for n from 2 to q do a1:=n*add(op(2,p)/op(1,p),p=ifactors(n)[2]);
%p A230543 a2:=a1*add(op(2,p)/op(1,p),p=ifactors(a1)[2]);
%p A230543 if type(sqrt(n^2+a1^2+a2^2),integer) then print(n);
%p A230543 fi; od; end: P(10^10);
